+The induction invariant is that the retained value is the minimum legal prefix Hamming cost for that exact key. If two prefixes share it, every common suffix encounters the same future local inputs, evolves through the same automaton labels, produces the same added bank increments and mismatch costs against the fixed public h, and reaches the same allowed terminal readout. Therefore the larger-cost prefix is dominated for a budget of at most k. Nonnegative mismatch increments justify pruning costs above k. In the unpruned scan, every legal full word corresponds to a path. Dominance pruning need not retain every such word; by the induction invariant it retains each reachable terminal key at its minimum cost with a legal witness. Evaluating U on these terminal keys and selecting its greatest exact value is therefore exact.
 
 This is not valid for exactly k edits, unless the actual cost is retained in the key (or a different correct recurrence is supplied). An additional additive nonnegative weighted history-dependent cost c(t,q,w,x;h) can replace the Hamming cost if its own budget is the intended threat model; to impose both Hamming and weighted constraints generally requires retaining an additional resource dimension or Pareto frontier. Arbitrary dependence on the *modified* full prefix cannot be silently handled by q: the stated q or w must actually make future increments and costs Markovian. Clean h is fixed read-only public information and may influence time-varying weights or costs. State restrictions may be imposed by marking transitions illegal or accepting only specified terminal q.
 
